---
/**
 * The landing page (U5: R1-R7).
 *
 * The security section states the primitives and the limits at the same
 * visual weight. That is deliberate: this is an unaudited, unsigned 0.1.0
 * asking people to trust it with production credentials, and a page that
 * mentions Argon2id loudly while whispering "unaudited" is selling something
 * it has not earned.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { DashboardReplica, VaultReplica, EnvReplica } from '../components/app/Replicas'

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL

const holds = [
  ['Credentials', 'Logins with usernames, URLs, notes and folders. Masked until you ask for them.'],
  ['Env files', 'One record per project and environment, kept byte for byte - comments and key order intact.'],
  ['Notes', 'Free-form notes with labels and favourites.'],
  ['Tasks', 'Due dates, overdue highlighting, completion.'],
  ['Income', 'Client payments with monthly and all-time totals.'],
  ['Calendar', 'Tasks and income laid out by month.'],
]
---

<Base
  title="Sanctum - an offline vault for freelance developers"
  description="Credentials, .env files, notes, tasks and income in one encrypted vault on your own machine. No account, no sync, no server."
>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Offline vault for Windows</p>
    <h1>Your client work, encrypted on your own machine.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Sanctum is a vault for freelance developers. Credentials, <code>.env</code> files, notes,
      tasks and income live in one encrypted file on your machine. There is no account, no sync,
      and no server &mdash; nothing to sign up for and nothing to breach.
    </p>
    <div class="heroActions">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={`${base}download/`}>Download for Windows</a>
      <a class="btn" href={`${base}guide/`}>Read the guide</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="stage wrap">
    <DashboardReplica />
    <p class="stageNote">
      Not a screenshot. This is rendered from the app&rsquo;s own design tokens, so it changes when
      the app does &mdash; try the theme switch above.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <h2>What it holds</h2>
    <p>
      A password manager holds your logins. Sanctum holds the rest of the job as well &mdash; the
      things a freelancer juggles across several clients at once.
    </p>
    <div class="grid">
      {holds.map(([name, detail]) => (
        <article class="tile">
          <h3>{name}</h3>
          <p>{detail}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <h2>The one it was built for</h2>
    <p>
      A <code>.env</code> is the file that actually carries the damage: the production database
      URI, the Stripe key, the AWS credentials. Sanctum stores it exactly as written, shows each
      key masked, and copies one value or the whole file through a clipboard that Windows history
      and cloud sync never see.
    </p>
    <div class="showcase">
      <EnvReplica />
      <VaultReplica />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <h2>How it is protected, and what that does not cover</h2>
    <p>
      Both halves belong on the same page. The first is what the software does; the second is what
      it cannot do for you.
    </p>
    <div class="twoUp">
      <article class="tile">
        <h3>The protection</h3>
        <ul class="plainList">
          <li>Your passphrase is stretched with Argon2id, calibrated on first run to your hardware.</li>
          <li>Each field is encrypted with AES-256-GCM under a fresh nonce.</li>
          <li>Every ciphertext is bound to its own row and column, so a moved value fails rather than authenticating somewhere else.</li>
          <li>Everything lives in one local SQLite file. The app makes no network request unless you turn website icons on.</li>
          <li>Copies are kept out of Windows clipboard history and cloud sync, and cleared automatically.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="tile tile--warn">
        <h3>The limits</h3>
        <ul class="plainList">
          <li><strong>Unaudited.</strong> The constructions are conventional, but no third party has reviewed them.</li>
          <li><strong>Unsigned.</strong> Installers carry no Authenticode signature, so Windows will warn you.</li>
          <li><strong>Windows only.</strong> The clipboard protections use Windows-specific formats.</li>
          <li><strong>No sync.</strong> Your vault exists on one machine. Take backups.</li>
          <li><strong>Lose the passphrase and the recovery code, and the data is gone.</strong> There is no reset, no email, and no backdoor. That is the design, and it cuts both ways.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap closing">
    <h2>Get it</h2>
    <p>
      Version 0.1.0, for Windows 10 and 11. The download page shows the checksum and explains the
      SmartScreen warning you will see.
    </p>
    <div class="heroActions">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={`${base}download/`}>Download for Windows</a>
      <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/itzmerai/sanctum">Read the source</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
